<p>The property occupied by this Dutch Colonial–style house was part of the original 1875 plat for Lincoln, the settlement that became Mandan.</p>

<p>On November 19, 1907, {% person "cora-swanson" %}Cora Swanson{% endperson %}, wife of railroad employee {% person "herman-swanson" %}Herman Swanson{% endperson %}, acquired the lot and presumably began building the house. Most American houses of this style, which dates to the seventeenth century, are found in New York's Hudson River region.</p>

<p>According to local tradition, a powerful windstorm once shifted the house several feet from its foundation. Its weakened joints required so much bracing that, when electrical service was later installed, an electrician had to tunnel through the maze of supports.</p>

<p>A driveway entrance provided access to basement rooms without disturbing the main living area. It served as a separate entrance for household staff. During the early 1900s, the house had extra bedrooms and accommodated railroad employees from Jamestown and Dickinson whose work required overnight stays in Mandan.</p>

<p>Stories about Herman Swanson's later years recall that he became hard of hearing before losing his driver's license. He reportedly revved his car until he could feel the engine running, engaged the clutch, and drove out of the narrow driveway. Groove marks along the house were said to have guided him toward the street.</p>
